```csharp
Debug.Assert(OLD(_size) == Count);
```

The comment "@@@ The size of the list doesn't decrease by 1 if the object is null" is related to the Remove method in the ArrayList class. The `Remove` method removes the first occurrence of a specific object from the list. If the object is null, it will find the first null element in the list and remove it. The size of the list should not decrease by 1 if the object is null.

```csharp
public virtual void Remove(Object obj)
{
    int index = IndexOf(obj);
    if (index >= 0)
        RemoveAt(index);
}
```
This code snippet is checking if the object passed is null or not. If the object is found in the ArrayList, it removes it by calling `RemoveAt` method which decreases the size of the ArrayList by 1. If the object is null, it will find the first null object in the ArrayList and remove it. If null is not found, it will do nothing. So, the size of the ArrayList should not decrease by 1 in both cases. Therefore, the assert statement is:

```csharp
Debug.Assert(OLD(_size) == Count || 
             (_items.Length > 0 && _items.Where((item, index) => index == _size - 1 && item == null).Count() > 0 && OLD(_size) == Count + 1));
```
This assert statement checks the condition that the size of the list remains unchanged unless a null element is being added to the list.
